


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: eipert
@title: Probabilistic Approaches to Repertoire and Melody in Medieval Chant
@presenter: eipert
@abstract: 

	Medieval chant survives in a vast and uneven record copied
	over many centuries. How this repertoire was transmitted
	remains a central question for musicology. Traditional
	scholarship addresses this question through close
	comparison of selected sources, from which musicologists
	develop hypotheses about shared lineages, regional
	dialects, and stylistic norms. Digital modelling can
	complement this work by making the assumptions behind such
	comparisons explicit and by scaling them to a larger
	portion of the surviving record. This talk presents two
	case studies that approach transmission from complementary
	angles: what manuscripts contain, and how their melodies
	are shaped. The first case study treats transmission as a
	question of community structure. A bipartite network links
	trope manuscripts to the trope elements they contain, and a
	nested stochastic block model infers groups of manuscripts
	that share repertoire. The resulting communities can then
	be plotted geographically and read against historical and
	political boundaries. The second case study treats
	transmission as a question of stylistic regularity. Using
	Bayesian generative models, competing hypotheses about what
	shapes a chant melody are formulated as explicit models and
	compared on parallel corpora of Introits and Tropes. Each
	model encodes a specific assumption about melodic
	structure, and the models can be evaluated against one
	another on the same data. Taken together, the two case
	studies point to a broader role for computational
	humanities: hypotheses that emerge from close reading can
	be reformulated as probabilistic models, compared against
	one another, and tested at the scale of an entire
	repertoire. Digital tools are valuable here not as a
	replacement for traditional musicological work, but as a
	way of taking its intuitions seriously enough to test them.

@@END: PRESENTATION



